"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 7.93. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pT2 stage with no extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion. Absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, which widens uncertainty as nodal sampling was not performed. Biopsy-to-prostatectomy differences are noted as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of nodal involvement documented in the pathology report. The primary uncertainty lies in the missing nodal sampling, which prevents definitive assessment of nodal status. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are critical unknowns that would significantly revise this prognosis. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data must be considered when assessing future risk. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 40.81–84.76 months; its coverage has not been validated."
